{#
_side_pane.html.twig — a pane docked beside the mail panes, with the handle
that resizes it. The calendar and the address book are both one of these.

Embedded, not included: the caller fills `side_pane_body`.

    {% embed '_partials/_side_pane.html.twig' with {
        name:        'contacts',                 data-side-pane, which app.css keys on
        open:        app.user.contactsPaneOpen,  drawn open on first paint
        widthVar:    '--contacts-pane-width',    the property ui--split writes
        width:       app.user.contactsPaneWidth, optional: sets widthVar here, for
                                                 a pane whose ui--split is this
                                                 wrapper rather than the shell
        handleLabel: 'contacts.pane.resize'|trans,
        attrs:       '…',                        raw attributes for the wrapper
        controller:  'ui--side-split',           optional: the boundary's
                                                 controller, for its targets and
                                                 actions (default ui--split)
    } %}

Placement is the whole point. This is a SIBLING of .main-pane, not a child, so
the two split the row's width and the handle between them moves the boundary:
one grows exactly as much as the other shrinks. .main-pane keeps flex-1 and
absorbs whatever is left, so each split is one number — the pane's width
property — and its handle only ever writes that. Two numbers would be two
sources of truth for one boundary.

The width reaches the pane through --side-pane-width, set on the wrapper from
the caller's own property. The pane's class has to name ONE property literally
for Tailwind to generate it, and each pane keeps its own stored width, so the
wrapper is where the two meet.

The width is rendered inline from the user's stored preference rather than
restored by JavaScript, so the first paint is already the right size. A pane
that appears at the default and jumps once the controller connects is worse
than one that does not remember at all.
#}

{% set controller = controller|default('ui--split') %}

{# Visibility is one conditional class rather than `lg:flex lg:hidden` — those
   two are the same breakpoint and which wins comes down to CSS source order,
   which is not something to rely on.

   hidden lg:flex when remembered open: the preference is one value shared by
   every device, and below lg a pane does not sit beside the mail. Opening it
   once at a desk should not mean every phone lands on it. #}
<div
    data-side-pane="{{ name }}"
    style="{% if width is defined %}{{ widthVar }}: {{ width }}px; {% endif %}--side-pane-width: var({{ widthVar }})"
    {{ attrs|default('')|raw }}
    class="{{ open ? 'hidden lg:flex lg:w-auto' : 'hidden' }} shrink-0 min-w-0"
>
    {# The handle. role=separator with aria-orientation is what makes it
       reachable and meaningful without a mouse; the arrow keys are handled by
       the controller. Its actions reach the pane's own boundary: the
       calendar's ui--split on the shell, the address book's ui--side-split on
       this wrapper.

       Two gutters wide and pulled left by one, so it spans the whole visual
       space between the panes rather than sitting to one side of it. The row's
       own gap-gutter falls to the LEFT of this wrapper, so a handle that was
       merely one gutter wide put its grip 1.5 gutters from the mail pane and
       0.5 from the pane — visibly off-centre, and a thin target. #}
    <div
        data-{{ controller }}-target="handle"
        data-action="pointerdown->{{ controller }}#start keydown->{{ controller }}#nudge dblclick->{{ controller }}#reset"
        role="separator"
        aria-orientation="vertical"
        aria-label="{{ handleLabel }}"
        tabindex="0"
        class="group relative -ml-gutter w-[calc(var(--spacing-gutter)*2)] shrink-0
               cursor-col-resize
               hidden lg:flex items-center justify-center
               focus:outline-none"
    >
        {# `.split-grip` is what the strain state in app.css grabs, so the two
           pixels of grip can say "there is more past this" while the pane is
           being dragged beyond its range. #}
        <span class="split-grip w-0.5 h-8 rounded-full bg-line
                     group-hover:bg-accent group-focus-visible:bg-accent" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </div>

    {# Width in classes rather than an inline style: inline would beat the
       breakpoint, and below lg the calendar's pane is full width rather than
       the stored one. #}
    <div
        data-{{ controller }}-target="pane"
        class="main-pane flex flex-col overflow-hidden min-w-0
               w-full lg:w-[var(--side-pane-width)]"
    >
        {% block side_pane_body %}{% endblock %}
    </div>
</div>
